Issue Summary
While building location options in packages/app-store/server.ts, the code iterates integrations and then checks for duplicates using array.find(...) on the per-category options array. That duplicate-check is done inside the loop and becomes a repeated O(n) scan, producing O(n²) behavior when many apps/credentials exist. This is a performance issue for environments with many installed apps/credentials.

Steps to Reproduce
1) Run the app locally or use a dev/staging environment of calcom/cal.diy.
2) Populate the database with many app credentials (e.g., >100 credentials across apps). You can seed or create many credentials via the App Store UI or DB seeds.
3) Trigger the server flow that returns location options (the function that builds locations in packages/app-store/server.ts). This is the server code that aggregates apps into categories and returns the location options.
4) Observe the endpoint latency or CPU while building the locations response.
(If you cannot seed many credentials, you can reproduce logically by mocking integrations with many credentials and calling the same function locally.)

Actual Results
The code uses apps[groupByCategory].find((o) => o.value === option.value) inside a loop to detect duplicates. For N options this yields repeated linear scans producing O(n²) behavior and increased CPU / latency under larger N.

Expected Results
The code should deduplicate options using O(1) membership checks (e.g., a Set or Map maintained per category) so the overall cost is O(n). Output should remain identical, only performance improved.

Technical details
File: packages/app-store/server.ts
Link: https://github.com/calcom/cal.diy/blob/main/packages/app-store/server.ts

Code example (existing pattern):
TypeScript
if (apps[groupByCategory]) {
  const existingOption = apps[groupByCategory].find((o) => o.value === option.value);
  if (!existingOption) {
    apps[groupByCategory] = [...apps[groupByCategory], option];
  }
} else {
  apps[groupByCategory] = [option];
}


Suggested fix: maintain a per-category Set<string> of seen option values (or an appsByCategory map that stores both options array and a seen Set). When adding an option, check seen.has(option.value) and only push/persist when not seen; add to seen afterwards.

Impact: converts repeated O(n) checks to O(1) membership tests; reduces worst-case operations from O(n²) to O(n) and improves latency/CPU when many apps/credentials are present.
Environment: reproducible locally; server-side change only (packages/app-store).

Evidence
- Observed repeated .find() use in code and other similar patterns in repo (e.g., typedInput.options?.find(...) in payment handling). No stack trace; this is a static-performance anti-pattern that shows up when inputs grow large.

﻿## Bug Description

createEventPbacProcedure is the tRPC middleware guarding team event type mutations (delete, host management, mass-apply). It delegates the actual permission check to a PermissionCheckService class defined inline in util.ts. That class is a stub that unconditionally returns 	rue:

`	s
class PermissionCheckService {
  constructor(_prisma?: unknown) {}
  async checkPermission(..._args: unknown[]) { return true; }
}
`

When a request targets a team event type (event.teamId != null), the middleware calls permissionCheckService.checkPermission(...) (line 162 of util.ts) and gets 	rue with no actual team-membership or role check. The tests in __tests__/util.test.ts lines 205 and 422 explicitly comment "PermissionCheckService stub always returns true, so org admin access is always granted" - confirming this is a known placeholder that was never replaced.

## Affected files

packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/eventTypes/util.ts, lines 15-18:

`	s
class PermissionCheckService {
  constructor(_prisma?: unknown) {}
  async checkPermission(..._args: unknown[]) { return true; }
}
`

packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/eventTypes/_router.ts, line 104:

`	s
delete: createEventPbacProcedure("eventType.delete")
  .input(...)
  .mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => deleteHandler({ ctx, input })),
`

All of these procedures are also affected: getHostsForAvailability, getHostsForAssignment, exportHostsForWeights, getChildrenForAssignment, getHostsWithLocationOptions, massApplyHostLocation.

## Failure scenario

1. Attacker authenticates as any valid cal.com user.
2. Calls iewer.eventTypes.delete with { id: <any_team_event_type_id> }.
3. Middleware fetches the event type, sees 	eamId is non-null, calls checkPermission(), receives 	rue.
4. deleteHandler deletes the event type without any further membership check.
5. Team event type IDs are sequential integers - trivially enumerable.

An attacker can delete all team event types across all organizations in the system.

## Fix

Replace the stub with a real implementation that checks team membership via Prisma before returning:

`	s
class PermissionCheckService {
  constructor(private prisma: PrismaClient) {}
  async checkPermission({ userId, teamId, fallbackRoles }: PermissionCheckArgs) {
    const membership = await this.prisma.membership.findFirst({
      where: { userId, teamId, accepted: true },
    });
    return membership != null && fallbackRoles.includes(membership.role);
  }
}
`

## Environment

cal.com main branch (2026-08-13).

## Issue Summary

Availability computation treats an all-day invitation from a third party as busy even when the user has never responded to it. On Apple Calendar (CalDAV) this silently removes entire days from the booking page.

The `TRANSP` check in `BaseCalendarService.getAvailability` is the only filter applied, so any `TRANSP:OPAQUE` event blocks — including invitations still awaiting a reply.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Connect an Apple Calendar (CalDAV) account and enable it under Settings → Calendars → check for conflicts.
2. Pick a day that is inside your booking window, on a working weekday, with **no** events in any connected calendar. Confirm the booking page offers slots on that day.
3. Have a different person send you an **all-day** invitation for that day.
4. Do **not** respond to it — leave it pending.
5. Reload the public booking page.

## Actual behaviour

The entire day becomes unbookable.

## Expected behaviour

An invitation the user has not accepted should not consume availability. At minimum this should be configurable; arguably `NEEDS-ACTION` and `DECLINED` should never block.

## Evidence — controlled reproduction on production (cal.eu)

| phase | the test day |
|---|---|
| before | **bookable** |
| pending all-day invite, nothing else changed | **fully blocked** |
| after declining the invite | **bookable again** |

Confounds ruled out for that day: no events in either connected calendar (Google Calendar and Microsoft 365), inside configured working hours on a working weekday, 8 days out so no minimum-notice interaction, well inside the rolling booking window, buffers unchanged.

## Root cause

The payload iCloud returns for such an invitation (redacted, structurally verbatim):

```
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTENDEE;CN="Organizer";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="organizer@example.com";
  PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;ROLE=CHAIR:/aXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX/principal/
ATTENDEE;CN="Me";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="me@example.com";
  ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:/aYYYYYYYYYYYYYYYY/principal/
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260728
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260729
ORGANIZER;CN="Organizer";EMAIL="organizer@example.com":/aXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX/principal/
SUMMARY:Test
TRANSP:OPAQUE
X-APPLE-CREATOR-IDENTITY:com.apple.mobilecal
X-APPLE-NEEDS-REPLY:TRUE
END:VEVENT
```

Two Apple-specific details matter for any fix:

1. **The ATTENDEE value is an iCloud principal path, not a `mailto:` URI.** The address appears only in the `EMAIL=` parameter. Any matcher keyed on `mailto:<email>` will not find the user.
2. **There is no `PARTSTAT` parameter on the invitee's line at all.** Apple sets PARTSTAT on the organizer but omits it for someone who hasn't replied — the absence is the signal, reinforced by `X-APPLE-NEEDS-REPLY:TRUE`. Per RFC 5545 §3.2.12 the default when absent is `NEEDS-ACTION`.

## Suggested fix

In the CalDAV availability path, skip events where the user's own participation status is `NEEDS-ACTION` or `DECLINED`:

```ts
const firstParam = (raw: string | string[] | undefined) =>
  Array.isArray(raw) ? raw[0] : raw;

const identities = [
  this.credential.user?.email,
  // For iCloud the CalDAV login is the Apple ID, which is frequently NOT the
  // Cal.com account email — whatever field holds the CalDAV username belongs here.
]
  .filter((e): e is string => Boolean(e))
  .map((e) => e.toLowerCase());

if (identities.length) {
  const organizer = vevent.getFirstProperty("organizer");
  const organizerEmail = String(
    firstParam(organizer?.getParameter("email")) ?? ""
  ).toLowerCase();
  const organizerValue = String(organizer?.getFirstValue() ?? "").toLowerCase();
  const iAmOrganizer = identities.some(
    (id) => organizerEmail === id || organizerValue === `mailto:${id}`
  );

  if (!iAmOrganizer) {
    const myAttendee = vevent.getAllProperties("attendee").find((prop) => {
      const rawValue = prop.getFirstValue();
      const value = typeof rawValue === "string" ? rawValue.toLowerCase() : "";
      const emailParam = String(
        firstParam(prop.getParameter("email")) ?? ""
      ).toLowerCase();
      return identities.some((id) => value === `mailto:${id}` || emailParam === id);
    });

    if (myAttendee) {
      // RFC 5545 §3.2.12: PARTSTAT defaults to NEEDS-ACTION when absent,
      // and parameter values are case-insensitive.
      const partstat = String(
        firstParam(myAttendee.getParameter("partstat")) ?? "NEEDS-ACTION"
      ).toUpperCase();
      if (partstat === "NEEDS-ACTION" || partstat === "DECLINED") return;
    }
  }
}
```

Four points that a naive implementation gets wrong, all verified against the payload above using ical.js 2.2.1:

- match on the `EMAIL=` parameter as well as the `mailto:` value (RFC 6638 §7.5)
- treat a missing `PARTSTAT` as `NEEDS-ACTION` (RFC 5545 §3.2.12)
- uppercase before comparing; parameter values are case-insensitive
- skip the check when the user is the ORGANIZER, so their own events stay busy

`ACCEPTED` and `TENTATIVE` continue to resolve to busy; self-created events (no ATTENDEE) are unaffected.

Test fixtures should be built from a real iCloud response rather than hand-written `mailto:` strings — hand-written fixtures pass even when the matcher never fires against real Apple data.

## Environment

- Instance: cal.eu (EU-hosted)
- Plan: Individuals (free)
- Connected calendars: Apple Calendar (CalDAV), Google Calendar, Microsoft 365
- Related: an implementation attempt exists at #29415, which does not handle either of the two Apple-specific details above

### Issue Summary

`sanitizeAndFilterGuests()` in `packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/addGuests.handler.ts` deduplicates guests by their **base email** (plus-addressing stripped), but it does that in two places that disagree about which duplicate wins.

`deduplicateGuestEmails()` keeps the **first** occurrence:

```ts
function deduplicateGuestEmails(guests: string[]): string[] {
  const seenBaseEmails = new Set<string>();
  return guests.filter((guest) => {
    const baseEmail = extractBaseEmail(guest).toLowerCase();
    if (seenBaseEmails.has(baseEmail)) return false;
    seenBaseEmails.add(baseEmail);
    return true;
  });
}
```

…while `emailToGuestMap` is built with `new Map(...)`, where a later entry **overwrites** an earlier one:

```ts
const emailToGuestMap = new Map(
  guests.map((guest) => [extractBaseEmail(guest.email).toLowerCase(), guest])
);
...
return uniqueGuestEmails
  .map((email) => emailToGuestMap.get(extractBaseEmail(email).toLowerCase()))
```

So the surviving *email* comes from the first duplicate, but the guest **object** that gets looked up is the last one. When they differ, the wrong person is added.

### Actual results

Adding two guests whose base emails collide:

```js
guests = [
  { email: "alice+work@x.com", name: "Alice Work" },
  { email: "alice+home@x.com", name: "Alice Home" },
];

deduplicated  -> ["alice+work@x.com"]                              // first wins
emailToGuestMap -> { "alice@x.com" => { email: "alice+home@x.com", name: "Alice Home" } }  // last wins
result        -> [{ email: "alice+home@x.com", name: "Alice Home" }]
```

The guest that survives deduplication is `alice+work@x.com`, but the object returned — and therefore the `name`, `email`, `timeZone` and `language` written to the booking by `addGuestsHandler` → `updateBookingAttendees` — belongs to `alice+home@x.com`.

`packages/features/bookings/services/BookingAttendeesService.ts:103` calls the same function.

The mismatch also affects the plain-duplicate case: sending the *same* base email twice with different display names silently keeps the second name while reporting the first address.

### Expected results

The guest object that is returned is the one whose email survived deduplication. One consistent tie-break — first occurrence wins, matching `deduplicateGuestEmails` — used in both places.

### Reproduction

```js
const extractBaseEmail = (e) => { const [l, d] = e.split("@"); return `${l.split("+")[0]}@${d}`; };

const guests = [
  { email: "alice+work@x.com", name: "Alice Work" },
  { email: "alice+home@x.com", name: "Alice Home" },
];

const map = new Map(guests.map((g) => [extractBaseEmail(g.email).toLowerCase(), g]));
map.get("alice@x.com");  // { email: 'alice+home@x.com', name: 'Alice Home' }  <- second one
```

### Related

While reading this: `extractBaseEmail()` in `packages/lib/extract-base-email.ts` returns the string `"noatsign@undefined"` for an input with no `@`, because `email.split("@")` yields a single element and `domain` is `undefined`. That value then flows into the blacklist comparison and the user lookup. It's a separate, smaller issue — happy to file it on its own if you'd prefer.

I have a fix ready and will open a PR referencing this issue.

### Issue Summary

`truncateOnWord()` in `packages/lib/text.ts` ignores the `maxLength` argument it is given and always cuts at a hard-coded `148`. On top of that, when the truncated slice contains **no space**, `lastIndexOf(" ")` returns `-1` and the whole string is thrown away, so the function returns literally `"..."`.

This is reachable from production: `apps/web/app/_utils.tsx` calls `truncateOnWord(description, 158)` in both `_generateMetadataWithoutImage` and `_generateMetadataForStaticPage`, which feed `openGraph.description` for **63 pages** across the web app, and the same string is passed on to the OG image generator.

```ts
export const truncateOnWord = (text: string, maxLength: number, ellipsis = true) => {
  if (text.length <= maxLength) return text;

  // First split on maxLength chars
  let truncatedText = text.substring(0, 148);   // <- maxLength is ignored

  truncatedText = truncatedText.substring(0, Math.min(truncatedText.length, truncatedText.lastIndexOf(" ")));
  //                                                                       ^ -1 when there is no space

  if (ellipsis) truncatedText += "...";

  return truncatedText;
};
```

### Actual results

Running the current implementation:

| input | `maxLength` | result |
|---|---|---|
| 580-char English text | 158 | 147 chars (not 158) |
| 360-char Japanese text | 158 | `"..."` |
| 360-char Korean text | 158 | `"..."` |
| a long URL with no spaces | 158 | `"..."` |

So for **any language that does not separate words with spaces** — Japanese, Korean, Chinese, Thai — every long meta description on those 63 pages collapses to a bare `...` in `<meta property="og:description">` and in the generated OG image.

### Expected results

- The cut respects the `maxLength` that was passed in.
- When there is no word boundary to break on, fall back to a hard character cut instead of discarding the entire string.
- The result never exceeds `maxLength` (today the ellipsis is appended *after* the cut, so the returned string can be longer than requested).

### Reproduction

```js
const truncateOnWord = (text, maxLength, ellipsis = true) => {
  if (text.length <= maxLength) return text;
  let truncatedText = text.substring(0, 148);
  truncatedText = truncatedText.substring(0, Math.min(truncatedText.length, truncatedText.lastIndexOf(" ")));
  if (ellipsis) truncatedText += "...";
  return truncatedText;
};

console.log(truncateOnWord("予約ページの説明文です。".repeat(30), 158)); // "..."
console.log(truncateOnWord("Book a meeting with our team ".repeat(20), 158).length); // 147, not 158
```

`truncateOnWord` currently has no test coverage — `packages/lib/text.test.ts` only exercises `truncate`.

I have a fix ready and will open a PR referencing this issue.

